    no backend, no tests.

  So there is no task model, no persistence, no user concept, and nothing that emits "a task changed." A notifications
  system is downstream of all four. I can't design "notify users when tasks they care about change" without knowing what
  a task is, what "care about" is recorded against, and what stores the change. That's not a detail I can assume past —
  different answers produce completely different systems (a client-side toast in one file vs. a server with a user
  table, a subscription model, and an email/push delivery pipeline).

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this repo is the real starting point or whether there's an existing tasks app this plugs into.

  - [1mEmpty page is the starting point[0m — then "notifications" isn't the first project. We'd need to decompose: task model
    + persistence first, then the change/event layer, then notifications on top. I'd brainstorm the first piece now and
    notifications gets its own cycle later. Slower to reach notifications, but nothing gets built twice.
  - [1mA real app exists elsewhere[0m — then point me at it and this becomes a normal architectural design against real code:
    I read its task model, its user model, and its persistence, and design notifications to fit. Much of what I'd
    otherwise guess becomes something I can read.
  - [1mThis repo is a prototype/demo[0m — notifications are the point, the task data can be fake and local. Then it's a small,
    honest single-page thing and most of the questions below vanish.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the second, if it's true — designing a notifications system against a real task model is
  dramatically cheaper than inventing one. If the empty page really is the start, I'd rather tell you now that
  notifications is a later project than design a subsystem onto a foundation that doesn't exist.
